reply:
Fixed `fetchAll` in `loader.mjs`. It returned before any fetch finished. `ids.forEach(async …)` does not wait for the async callbacks, so `fetchAll` returned `results` while it was still empty. The pushes happened later, after the widget had already read the list. That explains why the API calls succeeded but no users showed.

There was a second bug: `push` added results in the order fetches finished, not in the order of `ids`. That broke the documented ordering.

Fix:
```js
export async function fetchAll(ids, fetchOne) {
  return Promise.all(ids.map((id) => fetchOne(id)));
}
```
`Promise.all` waits for every fetch and keeps results in the same order as `ids`. Fetches still run in parallel. If any fetch rejects, `fetchAll` now rejects too. Before, that error was swallowed as an unhandled rejection.

I added a regression test to `loader.test.mjs` where fetches finish out of order. It checks that `fetchAll` waits for all of them and keeps the order. `npm test` passes all 4 tests. I did not run this test against the old code. `users.mjs` (`loadUsers`) needs no change.

